---
const editorNative = [
  {
    label: "Send to Agent",
    body: "Pass the current selection or the active file to the agent explicitly, with one shortcut — not by pasting content back and forth.",
  },
  {
    label: "Permission rules",
    body: "Reads and searches can stay frictionless while writes, shell commands, web access, and MCP calls ask by default. You set the boundaries.",
  },
  {
    label: "Undo / Redo",
    body: "Agent edits receive snapshots, so you can undo or redo them during the session without leaving the editor.",
  },
  {
    label: "LSP diagnostics",
    body: "After an edit, syntax and type errors from Sublime's LSP feed back to the agent — the editor itself stays part of the loop.",
  },
  {
    label: "Scoped modes",
    body: "Code, Architect, Ask, and Debug modes give each step a different toolset, instead of one always-maximal configuration.",
  },
  {
    label: "Visible costs",
    body: "Provider, model, reasoning effort, context, and per-session cost statistics are always one status bar away.",
  },
];
---

<section id="why-limitcode" class="why-lc">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="section-num reveal">02 — Why Limitcode</span>
      <h2 class="reveal d1">Why not just use Claude Code, Codex, Pi or OpenCode in a terminal?</h2>
      <p class="reveal d2">
        Terminal agents are excellent for terminal-first workflows — and Limitcode
        is not trying to replace them. It is built for developers who want the
        agent to live where they already work: inside Sublime Text.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="why-grid">
      <article class="why-card reveal d1">
        <h3>If your workflow is terminal-first, keep it.</h3>
        <p>
          Running a coding agent in a shell, next to any editor, is a proven way
          to work. Claude Code, Codex, Pi, and OpenCode do that well, and
          Limitcode does not attempt to re-create every terminal-first workflow.
        </p>
        <p>
          Limitcode exists for the other case: you want the agent inside the
          editor you already have open — reading the project on disk, applying
          approved edits, running approved commands — without adopting a
          terminal-centric loop. A realistic setup is Limitcode as the
          lightweight default agent inside Sublime, while terminal agents stay
          available for the specialized workflows that call for them.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="why-card reveal d2">
        <h3>More than a terminal embedded in Sublime.</h3>
        <p>
          The chat is a native Sublime view working against the project on disk,
          not a wrapped shell. That shows up in the details:
        </p>
        <ul class="why-list">
          {editorNative.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{item.label}.</strong> {item.body}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .why-lc {
    padding-top: clamp(56px, 7vw, 96px);
    padding-bottom: clamp(56px, 7vw, 96px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .why-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 18px;
    margin-top: clamp(36px, 5vw, 60px);
  }

  .why-card {
    padding: clamp(26px, 3.5vw, 40px);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    background: var(--paper-deep);
  }

  .why-card h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    max-width: 24ch;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .why-card p {
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  .why-card p + p {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }

  .why-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-top: 18px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .why-list li {
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .why-list li strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .why-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
